        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The commander either knew or, owing to the circumstances, should have known about the murders, rapes, and pillaging committed by MLC troops.",
      "reasoning": "Article 28(a)(i) imposes liability when the commander \"either knew or, owing to the circumstances at the time, should have known\" of the crimes. The record shows that consistent information about the crimes was brought to his attention, he initiated inquiries, and other MLC commanders exercised disciplinary authority, establishing that the commander had actual or constructive knowledge. The same knowledge standard is reflected in Customary IHL, Art. 86(2) of Additional Protocol I, which requires that a superior have information that should have enabled them to conclude the troops were committing or about to commit a breach.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "28(a)(i)",
          "quoted_text": "That military commander or person either knew or, owing to the circumstances at the time, should have known that the forces were committing or about to commit such crimes;"
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "customary_ihl",
