          "quoted_text": "if they knew, or had information which should have enabled them to conclude in the circumstances at the time, that he was committing or was going to commit such a breach and if they did not take all feasible measures within their power to prevent or repress the breach."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The commander failed to take all necessary and reasonable measures within his power to prevent or repress the crimes, thereby satisfying the second element of Article 28(a).",
      "reasoning": "Article 28(a)(ii) requires that the commander \"failed to take all necessary and reasonable measures within his or her power to prevent or repress\" the commission of crimes or to refer them to competent authorities. Although the commander sent a letter to CAR authorities and requested an investigation, the Tribunal found that MLC troops continued committing crimes throughout the operation and that the commander’s measures were insufficient given his effective control. The standard mirrors that of Additional Protocol I, Art. 86(2), which demands “all feasible measures within their power” to prevent or repress a breach.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "28(a)(ii)",
          "quoted_text": "That military commander or person failed to take all necessary and reasonable measures within his or her power to prevent or repress their commission or to submit the matter to the competent authorities for investigation and prosecution."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "customary_ihl",
          "article": "86(2)",
          "quoted_text": "if they did not take all feasible measures within their power to prevent or repress the breach."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The commander’s limited steps—requesting a CAR investigation and sending a letter—do not constitute “all necessary and reasonable measures” required by Article 28(a)(ii).",
      "reasoning": "Article 28(a)(ii) obliges a commander to take \"all necessary and reasonable measures\"; mere diplomatic correspondence or reliance on host‑state investigations falls short when the commander possesses effective authority over the troops. The ICC’s jurisprudence interprets this clause as requiring proactive, effective actions (e.g., ordering arrests, disciplining perpetrators, halting operations) rather than passive reliance on external authorities. The factual record shows that despite the commander’s inquiries, crimes persisted, indicating that the measures taken were not sufficient to meet the statutory threshold.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "28(a)(ii)",
          "quoted_text": "That military commander or person failed to take all necessary and reasonable measures within his or her power to prevent or repress their commission or to submit the matter to the competent authorities for investigation and prosecution."
        }
      ]
    }
